{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-94706","model_call":19,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ euthanasia (+ \"different cultures\" cultural cultures) (+ western eastern asian) (+ attitudes views))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ euthanasia (+ \"different cultures\" cultural cultures) (+ western eastern asian) (+ attitudes views))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_254128071#0_584256551","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q3204","rank":1,"snippet":"doc_55_254128071#0_584256551\" , \"end_char\": 991.000000 , \"headings\": \"In Ancient Rome, suicide was allowed as a form of euthanasia In Ancient Rome, suicide was allowed as a form of euthanasia Photo credit: Diliff \" , \"segment\": \"In Ancient Rome, suicide was allowed as a form of euthanasia In Ancient Rome, suicide was allowed as a form of <cover>euthanasia Jan 23, 2017 Domagoj Valjak Photo credit: Diliff Throughout history, various cultures had different attitudes toward suicide. For instance, in Victorian England suicide was a sin and a punishable crime: suicides were not allowed a proper burial and their property was confiscated by the state. In some Asian </cover>cultures, particularly in Japan, ritual suicides were sometimes considered honorable. High-ranking officials would often commit suicide after a particularly devastating mistake, in order to die with dignity and escape public disgrace. Bust of Titus Livius. Ancient Greece and Rome both had pretty lenient attitudes towards suicide. Ancient Roman civilization had a somewhat progressive approach which in some way resembled euthanasia. In Rome, suicide was not considered a crime or a sin against gods, and in "},"time":124},"model_calls":19,"document_judgments":14,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
