{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-94706","model_call":20,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":10032,"output_tokens":417,"total_tokens":10449,"cached_tokens":8960,"reasoning_tokens":229},"output_characters":895,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_55_254128071#0_584256551","relevance":2,"reason":"The article discusses historical and cultural variation in attitudes toward suicide and links ancient Roman suicide to euthanasia, with some East-West examples such as Victorian England and Japan, but it is not dedicated to euthanasia views and the evidence is indirect.","learned":"Historical attitudes toward self-chosen death varied: Victorian England treated suicide as sinful/criminal, while some Asian cultures such as Japan sometimes viewed ritual suicide as honorable; ancient Rome was more permissive."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ euthanasia buddhism (+ views ethics attitude) (+ christian western))","reason":"Search for Buddhist/Eastern views with Western/Christian comparison terms to retrieve focused evidence explaining Eastern versus Western moral frameworks on euthanasia."}}}
