{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-94706","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q3211"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#7_1051301998","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q3211","rank":16,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_489123501#7_1051301998\" , \"end_char\": 5670.000000 , \"headings\": \"Religious Views on Euthanasia Religious Views on Euthanasia Page Contents Buddhism Christianity Catholicism Protestantism Christians in support of <cover>euthanasia Hinduism Islam Jainism Judaism Shinto Unitarian Universalism Influence of religious views</cover>See also Adapted from Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Share this: Like this: Search Quote of the Day \" , \"segment\": \"It is forbidden for a Muslim to plan, or come to know through self-will, the time of his own death in advance. Jainism Main article: Sallekhana Jainism is based on the principle of non-violence ( ahinsa) and is best known for it. Jainism recommends voluntary death or sallekhana for both ascetics and srāvaka (householders) at the end of their life. Sallekhana (also known as Santhara, Samadhi-marana) is made up of two words sal (meaning ‘properly’) and lekhana, which means to thin out. Properly thinning out of the passions and the body is sallekhana. A person is allowed to fast unto death or take the vow of sallekhana only when certain requirements are fulfilled. "},"time":9},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":36,"relevance_3":19,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
